[{:db/id 17592186083307 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c76ce26c-3285-586a-b0c4-847ce7576f60" "question-879d12d0-2113-5e81-a5d2-2d55d4183634"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083107 :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 9x^4-3x^2-7x-6?$\n\nI. $-\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\nIII. $-9$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083306 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b75db326-1476-547f-a77b-b21ea5825d85"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083106 :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 12x^4+5x^3-6x+15?$\n\nI. $\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\nIII. $-\\dfrac{3}{4}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e7505886-69c0-558a-a441-b828788cc51e" "question-2e2ad5ba-576c-5412-b8cf-f6ef173b3a57"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b9c7c178-4ebe-5318-8cb7-742204e595a0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c76ce26c-3285-586a-b0c4-847ce7576f60" :question/id #uuid "c76ce26c-3285-586a-b0c4-847ce7576f60" :question/math-academy-id "q-83736" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+7x-8?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8,$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-4=-\\frac{4}{1}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(4)$ is a factor of $-8$ and the denominator $(1)$ is a factor of $5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73c01c85-76dc-5599-968a-5fa4f05c949e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73c01c85-76dc-5599-968a-5fa4f05c949e" :answer-field/id #uuid "73c01c85-76dc-5599-968a-5fa4f05c949e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-861251cb-82b1-5685-8d4f-6715f30f07d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-861251cb-82b1-5685-8d4f-6715f30f07d4" "answer-54d7c5c3-a9e2-524a-a7d5-fb4012f15478" "answer-316eb2af-0429-59de-b344-1be387d9a577" "answer-581646f9-25ed-5c5a-aeb0-c470516651f6" "answer-eb286b57-db1a-592b-8dcc-89415f746bae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-861251cb-82b1-5685-8d4f-6715f30f07d4" :answer/id #uuid "861251cb-82b1-5685-8d4f-6715f30f07d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54d7c5c3-a9e2-524a-a7d5-fb4012f15478" :answer/id #uuid "54d7c5c3-a9e2-524a-a7d5-fb4012f15478" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-316eb2af-0429-59de-b344-1be387d9a577" :answer/id #uuid "316eb2af-0429-59de-b344-1be387d9a577" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-581646f9-25ed-5c5a-aeb0-c470516651f6" :answer/id #uuid "581646f9-25ed-5c5a-aeb0-c470516651f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb286b57-db1a-592b-8dcc-89415f746bae" :answer/id #uuid "eb286b57-db1a-592b-8dcc-89415f746bae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083890 :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+17{x}^{2}-10x-9?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-9,$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-9}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{9}{2}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(9)$ is a factor of $-9$ and the denominator $(2)$ is a factor of $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082271 :answer-field/correct 17592186082273 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-879d12d0-2113-5e81-a5d2-2d55d4183634" :question/id #uuid "879d12d0-2113-5e81-a5d2-2d55d4183634" :question/math-academy-id "q-112864" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-3x+2?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $2,$ and the leading coefficient is $-4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }2}{\\text{factor of}\\,-4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(1)$ is a factor of $2$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b65fa8f3-11c1-5f2d-b151-e84007d15bf9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b65fa8f3-11c1-5f2d-b151-e84007d15bf9" :answer-field/id #uuid "b65fa8f3-11c1-5f2d-b151-e84007d15bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e742a257-c79d-5e7c-946b-347b8a6e55d8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c244dcdf-d702-5e3f-8cc2-53ceb79a1e2b" "answer-7b81c397-2f18-5ef5-b521-31182e577fe2" "answer-163d085f-a04f-5649-b71b-e90a4a64a76e" "answer-e742a257-c79d-5e7c-946b-347b8a6e55d8" "answer-1436fc42-169a-5d6e-8e67-e55cf95c9b4e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c244dcdf-d702-5e3f-8cc2-53ceb79a1e2b" :answer/id #uuid "c244dcdf-d702-5e3f-8cc2-53ceb79a1e2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b81c397-2f18-5ef5-b521-31182e577fe2" :answer/id #uuid "7b81c397-2f18-5ef5-b521-31182e577fe2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-163d085f-a04f-5649-b71b-e90a4a64a76e" :answer/id #uuid "163d085f-a04f-5649-b71b-e90a4a64a76e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e742a257-c79d-5e7c-946b-347b8a6e55d8" :answer/id #uuid "e742a257-c79d-5e7c-946b-347b8a6e55d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1436fc42-169a-5d6e-8e67-e55cf95c9b4e" :answer/id #uuid "1436fc42-169a-5d6e-8e67-e55cf95c9b4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b75db326-1476-547f-a77b-b21ea5825d85" :question/id #uuid "b75db326-1476-547f-a77b-b21ea5825d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-83824" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-7x+8?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-5$ does not take the above form. The value $-5$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{5}{1},$ and the numerator $(5)$ is not a factor of $8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-82b73eeb-28f6-544a-b4a5-e943633f0b85"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-82b73eeb-28f6-544a-b4a5-e943633f0b85" :answer-field/id #uuid "82b73eeb-28f6-544a-b4a5-e943633f0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b19fd5b-b890-57f9-8edb-92c8075ccdef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14dbd20f-dae6-536f-87a7-89593f714dee" "answer-8b19fd5b-b890-57f9-8edb-92c8075ccdef" "answer-7c44e16a-680d-5060-8d29-321f46d27bfa" "answer-e42f90f8-5654-57c3-8684-e319bb32b08d" "answer-d26b2ae3-d848-5cfd-a798-719a96527d83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14dbd20f-dae6-536f-87a7-89593f714dee" :answer/id #uuid "14dbd20f-dae6-536f-87a7-89593f714dee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b19fd5b-b890-57f9-8edb-92c8075ccdef" :answer/id #uuid "8b19fd5b-b890-57f9-8edb-92c8075ccdef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c44e16a-680d-5060-8d29-321f46d27bfa" :answer/id #uuid "7c44e16a-680d-5060-8d29-321f46d27bfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e42f90f8-5654-57c3-8684-e319bb32b08d" :answer/id #uuid "e42f90f8-5654-57c3-8684-e319bb32b08d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d26b2ae3-d848-5cfd-a798-719a96527d83" :answer/id #uuid "d26b2ae3-d848-5cfd-a798-719a96527d83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7505886-69c0-558a-a441-b828788cc51e" :question/id #uuid "e7505886-69c0-558a-a441-b828788cc51e" :question/math-academy-id "q-323225" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+8$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 8,\\text{ }\\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2f5acde8-ec70-5900-b2f5-c4bcc05cf9e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2f5acde8-ec70-5900-b2f5-c4bcc05cf9e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5acde8-ec70-5900-b2f5-c4bcc05cf9e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3b174001-a899-57c4-9bb5-2ae14ddc8f25" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b174001-a899-57c4-9bb5-2ae14ddc8f25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b174001-a899-57c4-9bb5-2ae14ddc8f25" :answer/id #uuid "3b174001-a899-57c4-9bb5-2ae14ddc8f25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2e2ad5ba-576c-5412-b8cf-f6ef173b3a57" :question/id #uuid "2e2ad5ba-576c-5412-b8cf-f6ef173b3a57" :question/math-academy-id "q-14143" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-18{x}^{2}+26x-12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-60877b9d-92c5-51c5-86d7-d416451d8505"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60877b9d-92c5-51c5-86d7-d416451d8505" :answer-field/id #uuid "60877b9d-92c5-51c5-86d7-d416451d8505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-087ad481-79d2-5803-a05b-415265f74035" :answer-field/choices ["answer-68ce9788-0470-5c3e-a1b2-2e1c81f8f0d9" "answer-d1b2ae26-7ce6-5b5d-8c0d-644be4a27793" "answer-087ad481-79d2-5803-a05b-415265f74035" "answer-cd3b1417-956a-5906-8f67-b13dacbb7ab9" "answer-cd8039a9-93f2-5720-ab0a-1873075b3cd6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68ce9788-0470-5c3e-a1b2-2e1c81f8f0d9" :answer/id #uuid "68ce9788-0470-5c3e-a1b2-2e1c81f8f0d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1b2ae26-7ce6-5b5d-8c0d-644be4a27793" :answer/id #uuid "d1b2ae26-7ce6-5b5d-8c0d-644be4a27793" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-087ad481-79d2-5803-a05b-415265f74035" :answer/id #uuid "087ad481-79d2-5803-a05b-415265f74035" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd3b1417-956a-5906-8f67-b13dacbb7ab9" :answer/id #uuid "cd3b1417-956a-5906-8f67-b13dacbb7ab9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd8039a9-93f2-5720-ab0a-1873075b3cd6" :answer/id #uuid "cd8039a9-93f2-5720-ab0a-1873075b3cd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b9c7c178-4ebe-5318-8cb7-742204e595a0" :question/id #uuid "b9c7c178-4ebe-5318-8cb7-742204e595a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-112977" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-3x+18$ has a root in the interval $(2,4),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $18$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }18}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(2,4)$ is $3.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-3$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-3)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x-3)(x+2)(x-3) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2,3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+3=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-91e3cfc1-8fc3-56d2-9a41-2e4cf39a8430"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-91e3cfc1-8fc3-56d2-9a41-2e4cf39a8430" :answer-field/id #uuid "91e3cfc1-8fc3-56d2-9a41-2e4cf39a8430" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-67e7348d-395b-5ab0-8891-b4eb9d1a0356" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9cb2fbb0-cb33-517d-ab50-a44b4aa0e364" "answer-285ade38-532c-552a-8139-54551e46a1fd" "answer-67e7348d-395b-5ab0-8891-b4eb9d1a0356" "answer-d6815b33-3bbe-52e5-a8e1-66282caf5f21" "answer-97c4e86d-95a3-53e3-b80a-a14798afdf4e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cb2fbb0-cb33-517d-ab50-a44b4aa0e364" :answer/id #uuid "9cb2fbb0-cb33-517d-ab50-a44b4aa0e364" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-285ade38-532c-552a-8139-54551e46a1fd" :answer/id #uuid "285ade38-532c-552a-8139-54551e46a1fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67e7348d-395b-5ab0-8891-b4eb9d1a0356" :answer/id #uuid "67e7348d-395b-5ab0-8891-b4eb9d1a0356" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6815b33-3bbe-52e5-a8e1-66282caf5f21" :answer/id #uuid "d6815b33-3bbe-52e5-a8e1-66282caf5f21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97c4e86d-95a3-53e3-b80a-a14798afdf4e" :answer/id #uuid "97c4e86d-95a3-53e3-b80a-a14798afdf4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234452 :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-479e5fb2-a0db-5547-90a9-3251c9634117"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-479e5fb2-a0db-5547-90a9-3251c9634117" :answer-field/id #uuid "479e5fb2-a0db-5547-90a9-3251c9634117" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b9344a36-f4c2-5ee5-8b24-5bc73e0761f9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b9344a36-f4c2-5ee5-8b24-5bc73e0761f9" "answer-19199503-6a7e-5f9f-a197-b117e0b7750b" "answer-d02dc8b0-f719-5d72-b3c5-684102a1c360" "answer-e264283e-cf89-55ac-b653-81b2eaae1082" "answer-bd738110-2730-510b-a6fe-9f8c39d2690d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9344a36-f4c2-5ee5-8b24-5bc73e0761f9" :answer/id #uuid "b9344a36-f4c2-5ee5-8b24-5bc73e0761f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19199503-6a7e-5f9f-a197-b117e0b7750b" :answer/id #uuid "19199503-6a7e-5f9f-a197-b117e0b7750b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d02dc8b0-f719-5d72-b3c5-684102a1c360" :answer/id #uuid "d02dc8b0-f719-5d72-b3c5-684102a1c360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e264283e-cf89-55ac-b653-81b2eaae1082" :answer/id #uuid "e264283e-cf89-55ac-b653-81b2eaae1082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd738110-2730-510b-a6fe-9f8c39d2690d" :answer/id #uuid "bd738110-2730-510b-a6fe-9f8c39d2690d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084456 :tutorial/content "The **rational roots theorem** (or **rational zeros theorem**) describes the rational roots of a polynomial with integer coefficients. The statement of the theorem is as follows:\n\n*Suppose that the rational number $\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{p}}}{\\color{red}{q}},$ in lowest terms, is a root of a polynomial with integer coefficients. Then $\\color{blue}p$ must be a factor of the polynomial's constant term, and $\\color{red}q$ must be a factor of its leading coefficient.*\n\nIn other words, given a polynomial with integer coefficients, any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = {\\color{red}{8}}x^3+12x^2+6x+{\\color{blue}{2}}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the rational roots theorem, any rational root of $f(x)$ must have a numerator that is a factor of $\\color{blue}{2}$ (that is, either $\\color{blue} \\pm1$ or $\\color{blue} \\pm2$) and a denominator that's a factor of $\\color{red} 8$ (${\\color{red} \\pm1},$ ${\\color{red} \\pm2},$ ${\\color{red} \\pm4},$ or $\\color{red} \\pm8$). \n\nAt first glance, it may seem like we have $16$ possible rational roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, if we reduce the above fractions to lowest form, we see that there are some duplicates, which we cancel out:\n\n![](images/91/91d3a123202f03ecec2d568548b5964fdc941e77d9edbad8ab386954bb046740.svg) \n\nRemoving the duplicates, we have ten *possible* rational roots. We list them from least to greatest in absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Watch out!** The above roots are only *possible* rational roots. They are *not* guaranteed to be roots of our polynomial $f(x).$ The only guarantee is that if $f(x)$ has any roots that are rational numbers, they are included in the list of numbers above. Here, it turns out that the only actual root among the possibilities is $-1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083447 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
